  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nPretty quiet forecast this week and into the early portion of\nthe weekend. Still a lot of uncertainty late in the forecast\nperiod.\n\nCurrently, surface low pressure over Saskatchewan with a warm\nfront extending southward through far western ND. The warm front\nwill push east through central ND by this evening. Over western\nND this afternoon westerly winds behind the cold front, combined\nwith continued dry air behind the weak cold front, will result \nin near critical fire weather conditions in far southwest ND for\na couple hours late this afternoon. This area is where winds \nare forecast to be strongest with the warmest temperatures. \nWinds are expected to diminish quickly early this evening. Lows \ntonight are expected to range from the lower 40s to lower 50s. \n\nTuesday will be noticeably cooler in the southwest, but much of\ncentral ND will see temperatures similar to today. Highs Tuesday\nwill range from the upper 60s to upper 70s. There may be another\nshort period of near critical fire weather conditions in the far\nsouthwest once again as we mix out in the mid to late afternoon.\nAt this time will hold off on adding the wording to the Fire \nWeather Forecast as the duration is short and the areal extent \nis even less than today (portions of Bowman County). Will let \nthe overnight shift take another look with a new model run.\n\nAs a northwest upper flow pattern becomes more established\nthrough mid-week, we see a very gradual downward trend in\nforecast high temperatures Tuesday through Thursday with \ndaytime highs on Thursday in the 60s statewide. A stray jet\ninduced sprinkle or shower is not out of the question, but will\nmaintain a dry forecast.\n\nFriday and Saturday looks to be a transition period from an\nnorthwest upper flow to a southwest upper flow. Surface low\npressure associated with a leading shortwave tracking from the\neastern Pacific to British Columbia Canada tracks into the\nNorthern Plains region by the Friday/Saturday timeframe. This\nshould allow for at least a little warmer temperatures into\nSaturday, although ensemble temperature spreads for \nFriday/Saturday have increased since yesterday so confidence is \nlow for a significant warm-up. There does look to be a cooldown\non Sunday with at least some low to moderate (20-40%)\nprecipitation chances as the aforementioned northern stream\nshortwave and attendant surface low push through the forecast\narea.\n\nThe following wave moving onshore results in a closed upper low\nover the Pacific Northwest by later in the day Saturday. There \nappears to be even more uncertainty in how this feature evolves \ninto early next week, when compared to yesterday. It does appear\nthat it will take longer for the closed low to eventually \nemerge in the Plains, and although there is still a colder \nsolution, the trend was toward a not so cold solution. There \nremains quite large spread in the NBM ensemble temperatures \nSunday and into next week. The deterministic models show pretty \ngood agreement in a closed upper low dropping south into the \nGreat Basin region, but with increasing uncertainty as to when \nand where they emerge onto the Plains. At this time will\nmaintain NBM temperatures and PoPs through the forecast period.",
